WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.342
A planner is useful when it supports a thinking routine.

2
00:00:03.542 --> 00:00:08.906
Name a specific outcome, such as writing a claim supported by two observations.

3
00:00:09.106 --> 00:00:12.393
Break the work into actions that a learner can begin.

4
00:00:12.593 --> 00:00:16.338
PlanSplat can organize assignments and study time.

5
00:00:16.538 --> 00:00:24.149
Brain Sort can turn a task dump into a checklist, Step-Splat can divide a large task and Clock Wizard can help

6
00:00:24.349 --> 00:00:25.790
estimate phases.

7
00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:28.676
A paper checklist is a valid alternative.

8
00:00:28.876 --> 00:00:33.897
During the work block, pause to compare the developing product with the criteria.

9
00:00:34.097 --> 00:00:39.796
Ask: have I named the location, included the evidence and explained the connection?

10
00:00:39.996 --> 00:00:45.550
A timer tells you that time passed; it does not tell you whether the explanation improved.

11
00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:48.042
Model the self-questioning aloud.

12
00:00:48.242 --> 00:00:54.450
If evidence is missing, change the next action instead of simply adding more decorative features.

13
00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:59.732
After the task, identify one improvement and the strategy that produced it.

14
00:00:59.932 --> 00:01:04.632
Schedule a later opportunity to explain the idea without the original scaffold.

15
00:01:04.832 --> 00:01:07.851
This makes the plan more than a list of due dates.

16
00:01:08.051 --> 00:01:14.033
Pause now and create two short work blocks for your course artifact, plus a later review.

17
00:01:14.233 --> 00:01:20.116
Keep the goal and criteria beside the schedule so that the plan remains connected to the learning.
